{%- comment -%}
  snippets/section-width.liquid
  ============================================
  See /guidelines.md ("Section-width pattern") for the full write-up —
  why this exists, the failure mode it prevents, and the step-by-step
  checklist for applying it to a new section. This file's own comments
  below cover usage; guidelines.md covers the reasoning and the running
  list of sections already migrated.
  ============================================
  CANONICAL REFERENCE SOURCE for the theme's three-way section width
  pattern (Default / Full width / Custom width).

  This is the ONLY place this logic should exist. Do not re-write the
  width_type/width_class assign or the --section-padding/--custom-width
  style lines inside an individual section file — render this snippet
  instead. Every section that needs Section width / Side padding
  controls should be a thin wrapper around this file, not a fork of it.
  (This is the fix for a recurring bug: sections that copy-pasted their
  own scoped/BEM version of this logic — including their own duplicate
  max-width CSS rules — stopped matching base.css's global
  .shopify-section > .default-width / .full-width / .custom-width
  rules, since those rules key off these exact class names. Seen twice
  now: main-header.css originally, product-information.css second.)

  OUTPUTS a class + style attribute pair, ready to drop directly onto
  an element:

    <div {% render 'section-width', section: section %}>

  If that element also needs a fixed class of its own (e.g. a section
  that has other CSS keyed to a stable class name, or a JS hook like
  data-product-information relying on a predictable selector), pass
  extra_class — it's merged into the same class attribute rather than
  requiring a second class="" on the element:

    <div {% render 'section-width', section: section, extra_class: 'product-information' %}>

    → class="product-information default-width" style="..."

  That element MUST be a direct child of .shopify-section — base.css's
  rules use a child combinator (`.shopify-section > .default-width`,
  etc.), so wrapping it in another element first breaks the match.

  REQUIRED SCHEMA SETTINGS — copy this block, unmodified, into any
  section that renders this snippet:

    { "type": "header", "content": "Section layout" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "section_width",
      "label": "Section width",
      "options": [
        { "value": "", "label": "Default" },
        { "value": "full-width", "label": "Full width" },
        { "value": "custom-width", "label": "Custom width" }
      ],
      "default": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "custom_width",
      "label": "Custom width",
      "min": 40, "max": 120, "step": 5, "unit": "rem", "default": 80,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.section_width == 'custom-width' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "section_padding",
      "label": "Side padding",
      "min": 0, "max": 80, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "default": 28
    }

  The setting ids (section_width, custom_width, section_padding) are
  fixed — this snippet reads them by name. Renaming them in one
  section's schema without updating this snippet breaks that section
  silently (falls back to the defaults below, no error thrown).
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign width_type = section.settings.section_width
  if width_type == blank
    assign width_class = 'default-width'
  else
    assign width_class = width_type
  endif
-%}class="{% if extra_class != blank %}{{ extra_class }} {% endif %}{{ width_class }}" style="--section-padding: {{ section.settings.section_padding | default: 28 }}px;{% if width_type == 'custom-width' %} --custom-width: {{ section.settings.custom_width | default: 80 }}rem;{% endif %}"
